course: everyday
lesson: 196
title: White lies and kind silence
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes the plain truth would hurt someone and a big lie would not be honest. There is a third way: find something TRUE and kind to say, or kindly say nothing at all.
explain: Example: Grandma knits you a jumper you do not like and asks if you like it. You can say «Thank you, it's so cosy, and you remembered my favourite colour!» Every word is true, and Grandma feels thanked for her effort.
explain: Not every opinion has to be said. Keeping quiet about a haircut or a lunch you would not choose is not lying; it is good manners. Private things can stay private too: «I'd rather not talk about that» is honest.
explain: Kind honesty matters most when someone will act on your answer: a friend about to sing at a talent show needs a gentle true tip, not a false «it's perfect». And happy secrets, like a surprise party, are fine because they end soon and make someone glad.
fact: You can thank someone warmly for their thought and effort even when you do not like the gift.
fact: Finding something true and kind to say is better than both a hurtful truth and a big lie.
fact: Kind silence is not a lie; not every opinion needs to be said.
fact: Honest, gentle encouragement helps people improve and keeps your word trustworthy.
fact: When someone will act on your answer, a false «it's perfect» can lead them into embarrassment later.
fact: A comforting truth, like «It was not your fault», is the best kind of kindness.
fact: A surprise is a happy secret that ends soon and is meant to make someone glad.
fact: Keeping private things private is your right and is not lying.
fact: Families decide how to handle traditions like the tooth fairy or Father Christmas, so do not spoil them for younger children.
fact: Remarks about someone's food can make them ashamed, because food comes from different families and cultures.
fact: A harmless secret shared in trust, like a friend's crush, should be kept.
fact: Some people find their age or weight private, and it is polite not to press.
fact: When grown-ups call someone a «friend» in a special way, or say two people are «more than friends», they usually mean a boyfriend or girlfriend, a couple; that is their private business.
exercise: Grandma gives you a jumper you do not like and asks if you like it. What is the best answer? | «Thank you, it's so cosy and you remembered my favourite colour» | «No, I don't like it, it's ugly» | «I love it more than anything I own and I'll wear it every single day» | «Can we take it back to the shop?»
exercise: A friend shows you a drawing and asks if it is good. What should you do? | Say what you truly like about it, and maybe one gentle idea | Say it is the best drawing in the whole world, even if it is not | Say it is bad so she tries harder | Say nothing and walk away from her
exercise: Someone has a new haircut you do not like. Must you give your opinion? | No, kind silence is fine and is not a lie | Yes, you must always say what you think | Yes, otherwise you are telling a lie | No, but you should tell others instead
exercise: Your aunt's cooking does not taste nice to you. What is polite and true? | Thank her for making dinner and eat what you can | Tell everyone at the table it tastes bad | Say it is the best meal you have ever had in your whole life | Push the plate away without a word
exercise: A friend's hamster died and she asks if it was her fault, but it was not. What do you say? | Tell her honestly it was not her fault and you are sorry | Say maybe it was, to be completely honest | Change the subject quickly to something more cheerful, like the weekend | Tell her hamsters do not matter very much
exercise: You are planning a surprise party and the birthday girl asks what you are doing on Saturday. What is fine? | Keep the surprise, for example «It's a secret!» | Tell her all about the party right away so she can choose her outfit | Refuse to ever speak to her again | Tell her you are moving to another town
exercise: A friend will sing at the talent show tomorrow and asks if her singing is good. One part is shaky. What helps her most? | «You're brave to do it, maybe practise the high part once more» | «It's perfect, don't change anything at all» | «You'll be terrible, don't go on stage» | «I don't really care about singing, you should ask someone else instead»
exercise: Someone asks about something private you do not want to share. What can you say? | «I'd rather not talk about that» | Make up a false story to end the question | Tell them everything so you are honest | Shout at them for being much too nosy
exercise: Your younger cousin believes in the tooth fairy. What should you do? | Not spoil it, and leave it to her family | Tell her the truth right away | Laugh at her for believing it | Show her where the coins really come from so she knows the truth
exercise: A classmate's lunch smells unusual to you. What should you do? | Keep the comment to yourself | Tell the whole table it smells odd | Ask her to eat somewhere else | Hold your nose so she notices
exercise: You were a bit bored at a friend's party and she asks if you had fun. What is true and kind? | «Thanks for inviting me, the cake was great» | «It was honestly the best day of my whole life» | «No thanks, it was really boring, to be honest» | «I only came along because of the presents, really»
exercise: Your dad asks if you like his new car and you do not care much. What is fine? | «It's nice, I like the colour» | «Cars are boring, I don't care» | «It's even worse than our old one» | «I'll only like it if it's red»
exercise: A friend tells you about her crush and asks you to keep it quiet. What should you do? | Keep it, because it is a harmless secret shared in trust | Tell the boy so he knows | Tell the class so it is fair | Keep it only until lunchtime, then it is fine to tell people
exercise: Someone asks a lady how old she is and she looks uncomfortable. What is polite? | Do not press or repeat the question | Ask her again and again until she finally tells you the answer | Guess her age out loud in front of others | Ask someone else how old she really is
test: A boy in Dublin gets socks he finds boring from his uncle. Which reply is both honest and kind? | «Thanks, it was really kind of you to think of me» | «These are the coolest socks ever made» | «I already have far too many socks, so you can keep these» | «Socks aren't a real present»
test: Which of these is kind silence and not a lie? | Not mentioning that you dislike a friend's new shoes | Saying you love the shoes when you hate them | Telling others her shoes are ugly | Saying you have the very same shoes at home when you don't
test: Why is a surprise party secret okay but a secret an adult says to keep forever is not? | A surprise ends soon and makes someone happy | Party secrets are short and all others are long | Adults are never allowed to keep any secrets at all | Any secret at all is fine if it is about a party
test: A girl in Perth is about to read her poem at assembly and asks your opinion. One line is hard to hear. What is best? | Praise what is good and suggest she read that line louder | Tell her it is completely flawless so that she feels confident | Tell her not to read it at all | Say you are too busy to listen to it
test: Your friend's dog ran away while you were walking it together, and she asks if it was her fault. It was not. Why should you say so? | A comforting truth helps her and is honest | Because you should always blame yourself | Because the truth never matters to friends | Because she will never ask again
test: A classmate keeps asking why your parents live apart. What can you honestly do? | Say you would rather not talk about it | Invent a story about it so that he stops asking you questions | Tell him absolutely everything to be polite | Pretend you did not hear him ask anything
test: Why can a false «it's perfect» be unkind? | The person may act on it and be embarrassed later | Because praise always makes people lazy | Because nobody really likes to hear good things about their work | Because it is always rude to be nice
test: A new boy brings rice and fish for lunch that smells strong to you. What is the kind choice? | Say nothing about it, since families eat different foods | Ask him loudly why he eats such strange food instead of sandwiches | Warn the others to sit far away | Tell the teacher the smell is a problem
test: nan knitted me an ugly jumper, what do i even say | Thank her warmly for the thought and effort | Tell her it's ugly | Say you'll wear it every day, then hide it at the back of a drawer | Give it straight back
test: my bro says santa isnt real, tell my lil sis? | No, families decide that, don't spoil it for her | Yes, she needs the truth now | Yes, and tell the whole class too, so everyone knows the truth | Only tell her on her birthday
test: mate's drawing is meh and she's gonna enter it in the contest | Give honest, gentle tips to help her improve | Tell her it's perfect so she feels good when she enters it | Tell her it's terrible | Say nothing and laugh
test: my mates crying, says the team lost cos of her, it wasnt tho | Tell her the truth kindly: it wasn't your fault | Agree it was her fault | Say nothing at all | Tell her to stop crying because the game doesn't matter anyway
explain: A girl in Leeds told her friend her new haircut looked fine when the friend asked, even though she did not love it; the friend was happy, and nothing bad came of a kind word.
explain: A boy in Texas kept "kind silence" about a friend's cuts on her arm because she asked him to; she was hurting herself, and only when he told a teacher did she get help.
fact: Kind silence is fine for small things like gifts, but not for anything that could hurt someone.
fact: If a friend is being hurt or hurting themselves, you tell a trusted adult even if they asked you not to.
test: gran's jumper is a bit itchy, tell her i hate it? | Thank her kindly for the effort, no need to say it | Tell her it's horrible | Throw it away in front of her so she doesn't knit another one | Give it back angrily
test: friend says don't tell, she's hurting herself | Tell a trusted adult, her safety matters more | Keep it secret, you promised, and promises always come first | Tell everyone at school | Ignore it
test: mate asks if his drawing's good, it's kinda meh | Say something kind and true, like a part you like | Say it's the worst ever | Laugh at it | Rip it up
test: did u hear Kate's haircut, apparently her mum did it with garden shears lol, everyone's saying she looks awful | Don't spread it; kind silence about looks is better than mean gossip | Spread it, it's only a joke | Tell Kate she looks awful | The garden shears part is surely true, so everyone ought to hear about it
test: my mate says Ava's got cuts on her arm but made him swear not to tell, people say she's just attention seeking | Don't gossip, but a trusted adult must know, her safety matters most | Keep it secret, he swore | Tell the whole class so that they can all keep an eye on her and help out | Ignore it, it's attention seeking
test: apparently Leo told his nan he loved her knitted jumper but he HATES it, he's such a liar right | Not really; thanking her warmly for her effort can be true and kind | Yes, he should go back and tell her that the jumper is ugly and itchy | He should give the jumper away | Lying to nans is always fine
test: auntie asked if mum's new "friend" is coming to xmas and mum went red and said "don't" — what's going on and what do i say? | Mum may have a new boyfriend she isn't ready to talk about; let her tell you | Mum is angry at the friend, so you should ask the friend to stay away at Christmas | Auntie means a work friend, so tell everyone at school | It's a secret game, so keep asking mum until she says
test: my mate asked "is it true ur big sister's got a boyfriend??" and she made me promise not to tell. what's kind and honest? | Say «that's her business, ask her» without lying or telling | Make up a fake name so nobody guesses | Tell the whole class so the secret's out and it's all done with | Say she's horrible and nobody would date her
test: gran whispered "those two are a bit more than friends, if you know what I mean" about the neighbours. what does she mean and what do you do? | They are probably a couple; that is their private business, so leave it | They are secretly enemies, so you warn them | They must be cousins, so you go and ask them all about their family tree | They broke a rule, so you tell the police
exam: Which of the following constitutes the recommended response upon receiving a gift one does not like? | Thank the giver warmly for their thought and effort | Tell the giver plainly that the gift is not liked | Say the gift is perfect and the best ever made | Return the gift without saying anything at all
exam: Identify the circumstance in which kind silence is NOT appropriate. | When someone is being hurt or is hurting themselves | When a friend's haircut is not to one's taste | When one does not like a lunch someone chose | When one is asked about a private family matter
exam: It is given that a friend will sing at a talent show and asks for an opinion. Determine the most suitable answer. | A gentle, true tip that helps them improve | A false assurance that it is perfect | A blunt remark that it is the worst | A refusal to say anything whatsoever
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning a surprise party. | It is a happy secret that ends soon and makes someone glad | It is a harmful lie that should be told at once | It is a promise that must never be revealed | It is private and must be kept forever
